---
import SoftwareDocsLayout from '../../../layouts/SoftwareDocsLayout.astro';
import { faarfieldDownload } from '../../../data/documentation';
import DocCallout from '../../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro';
import DocChecklist from '../../../components/docs/DocChecklist.astro';
import DocDecision from '../../../components/docs/DocDecision.astro';
import FaarfieldManual from '../../../components/docs/FaarfieldManual.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const reference = `${base}documentation/faarfield/reference/`;
const toc = ['Download', 'Start a job', 'Pavement', 'Traffic', 'Run', 'Results', 'Concepts', 'Exercises', 'Assumptions', 'Troubleshooting', 'User’s manual', 'Sources'].map(label => ({ id: label === 'User’s manual' ? 'manual' : label.toLowerCase().replaceAll(' ', '-'), label }));
const modes = [
  { value: 'design', label: 'Find a pavement thickness', title: 'Thickness Design', body: 'Enter the section, traffic mix, and design life. FAARFIELD varies the selected design layer. Check that this is the layer you intend to change.', href: `${reference}designmode/`, linkLabel: 'Thickness design steps' },
  { value: 'life', label: 'Check an existing section’s life', title: 'Life', body: 'Enter the section’s actual thicknesses and the traffic mix. Review the calculated structural life and its CDF results. This mode holds layer thicknesses fixed.', href: `${reference}pavementlifemode/`, linkLabel: 'Life analysis steps' },
  { value: 'compaction', label: 'Check subgrade compaction requirements', title: 'Life and Compaction', body: 'Use the completed section and traffic mix. Read the cohesive and non-cohesive soil tables in the Section Report, including their depth references and Proctor-test notes.', href: `${reference}lifeandcompactionmode/`, linkLabel: 'Compaction analysis steps' },
  { value: 'pcr', label: 'Report pavement strength', title: 'ACR/PCR', body: 'Use the pavement-strength reporting procedure and the relevant traffic inputs. A PCR result is a strength rating, not a pavement thickness or remaining-life estimate.', href: `${reference}pcrmode/`, linkLabel: 'ACR/PCR mode' },
];
---
<SoftwareDocsLayout title="FAARFIELD · CEE 406" description="A course guide to FAARFIELD, with the FAA help reference." activeModule="faarfield" toc={toc}>
  <header class="doc-header">
    <span class="doc-header__badge">Course guide</span>
    <h1 class="doc-header__title">FAARFIELD</h1>
    <p class="doc-header__desc">Design and evaluate flexible and rigid airport pavements.</p>
  </header>
  <section id="download" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Download</h2>
    <p>Get the installer and readme from the <a href={faarfieldDownload}>FAA download page</a>. The FAA recommends Windows 10 or newer. Follow the readme for installation.</p>
    <DocCallout label="Version" kind="info"><p>The FAA lists 2.1.2. Our reference comes from the supplied 2.1.1 help and retains some 2.0 labels. Use the version assigned in class and its readme. Download source checked October 1, 2026.</p></DocCallout>
  </section>
  <section id="start-a-job" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Start a job</h2>
    <ol class="doc-list">
      <li>Open FAARFIELD and select <strong>New Job</strong>.</li>
      <li>Name the job and its pavement section. A job can contain several sections.</li>
      <li>Save the job before entering your inputs. Use a separate section for each alternative you want to compare.</li>
      <li>Check <strong>Units</strong> in Design Options. Choose Metric (SI) or US Customary before entering values.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>See <a href={`${reference}jobsandjobfiles/`}>jobs and job files</a> and <a href={`${reference}units/`}>units</a>.</p>
    <DocCallout><p>Name sections by the change, such as “Base 250 mm.” Keep the original section as your baseline.</p></DocCallout>
  </section>
  <section id="pavement" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Pavement</h2>
    <p>Select the pavement type first: a new flexible or rigid section, or the appropriate overlay type. Enter the design life, layer materials, thicknesses, and the required material properties.</p>
    <p>For thickness design, check which layer the program will adjust. For a life analysis, enter the existing section thicknesses. Use the assignment values; library defaults are not a substitute for the specified inputs.</p>
    <p>See <a href={`${reference}definingpavementstructure/`}>pavement structure</a>, <a href={`${reference}materiallibrary/`}>materials</a>, and <a href={`${reference}designlayer/`}>design layer</a>.</p>
    <DocCallout label="Design layer" kind="note"><p>The selected design layer has a red arrow in the grid and a green outline in the section image. Confirm it before running a thickness design.</p></DocCallout>
  </section>
  <section id="traffic" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Traffic</h2>
    <p>Add each aircraft from the library. Check its model, gear configuration, gross weight, annual departures, and annual growth. Similar aircraft names can have different landing gear.</p>
    <p>Annual departures means departures in the first year of the design life, before growth. Keep design life and annual growth in their own fields.</p>
    <p>See <a href={`${reference}definingsectiontrafficmix/`}>traffic mix</a> and <a href={`${reference}viewingaircraftgearconfigurations/`}>gear configurations</a>.</p>
    <DocCallout label="Traffic check" kind="note"><p>Use departures, not total arrivals plus departures. Follow the assignment for any traffic adjustments. If one aircraft operates at distinct weights, keep the weight groups separate when specified.</p></DocCallout>
  </section>
  <section id="run" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Run</h2>
    <DocDecision id="faarfield-mode" label="What are you checking?" choices={modes} />
    <DocChecklist id="faarfield-run-v1" title="Before running" items={['The selected section and units are correct.', 'Layer materials, thicknesses, and properties match the assignment.', 'The design layer and analysis mode match the question.', 'Aircraft models, weights, first-year departures, and growth are checked.', 'The job is saved with a separate baseline section.']} />
    <p>Select the mode for the current section, check the inputs, and click <strong>Run</strong>. Read the Calculation Status tab for progress and messages. Rigid pavement calculations can take longer.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="results" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>Open the <strong>Section Report</strong> and <strong>CDF Graph</strong> from the Explorer. Check the final layer thicknesses, calculated life, traffic, and options before using the result.</p>
    <p>Cumulative damage factor (CDF) sums the fatigue damage from the traffic mix. CDF = 1 is the design model’s failure criterion for the mode being checked. Read the CDF profile across the pavement; the largest aircraft by weight may not control the design.</p>
    <DocCallout label="Read the total CDF" kind="note"><p>Aircraft can peak at different lateral offsets. Do not add each aircraft’s separate maximum and call it the total maximum. Use the total CDF curve. CDF above 1 means the modeled criterion is exceeded, not that the pavement immediately becomes unusable.</p></DocCallout>
    <p>Use <strong>Save As PDF</strong> to export reports and graphs. Save the job file too, so you can reopen the inputs. For a comparison, record what changed and keep the other inputs fixed.</p>
    <p>See <a href={`${reference}appendixbcumulativedamagefactor/`}>CDF</a>, <a href={`${reference}reportsandgraphs/`}>reports</a>, and <a href={`${reference}pdfoutputsofreportsandgraphs/`}>PDF output</a>.</p>
    <p>Design basis: <a href="https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/150-5320-6G-Pavement-Design.pdf">AC 150/5320-6G</a>, §3.12.9, explains CDF and its lateral distribution.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="concepts" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Concepts</h2>
    <details open><summary>Response, damage, and thickness</summary><p>A structural model computes load-induced stresses or strains. A failure relationship converts that response into allowable departures. Applied departures divided by allowable departures gives each aircraft’s damage contribution; contributions are combined at each lateral location. Thickness design iterates a selected layer until the governing damage criterion is met. Life analysis keeps the section fixed instead. See <a href={`${reference}appendixatheoreticalapproachtopavementdesignprocedures/`}>Appendix A</a> and <a href={`${reference}appendixbcumulativedamagefactor/`}>Appendix B</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Flexible and rigid pavement checks</summary><p>Flexible design checks vertical strain at the subgrade; the <strong>Calculate HMA CDF</strong> option also reports asphalt fatigue damage associated with horizontal strain. Check each HMA layer, including stabilized asphalt base. Rigid design uses concrete tensile stress and a fatigue relationship. A concrete modulus and flexural strength describe different properties; do not enter one in place of the other. Read <a href={`${reference}newflexibleandhmaonflexible/`}>flexible theory</a>, <a href={`${reference}newrigidpavement/`}>rigid theory</a>, and <a href={`${reference}calculatehmacdf/`}>HMA CDF</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Aggregate layers are not a single fixed modulus</summary><p>For library unbound aggregates such as P-209 and P-154, FAARFIELD internally subdivides the layer and assigns moduli recursively. The assignment depends on layer thickness and support below it. A change in base thickness can change both geometry and assigned stiffness. Do not expect a separate elastic analysis with one arbitrary base modulus to reproduce the same result. <a href={`${reference}appendixcmodulusassignmentprocedureforaggregatelayers/`}>Appendix C</a> gives the sublayer rules and equations.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Existing concrete condition</summary><p>Overlay design includes deterioration of the existing rigid pavement. Its Structural Condition Index (SCI) is not a general pavement condition score. Percent CDF Used describes consumed fatigue life and is not interchangeable with SCI. In the manual’s SCI = 70 example, percent CDF Used is fixed at 100. The existing PCC modulus shown in the report can be its undamaged reference value rather than the reduced value used during the calculation. See <a href={`${reference}pccoverlayonrigiddesignexample/`}>the overlay example</a>.</p></details>
  </section>
  <section id="exercises" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Exercises</h2>
    <p>Work through these with the supplied manual open. They are practice exercises, separate from homework. Save the inputs, options, software version, aircraft library date, and reports for each run.</p>
    <DocCallout label="Reproducing an example" kind="note"><p>Use the manual’s complete “Design Examples” traffic mix, including aircraft weights, departures, and growth. If it is absent from your installation, transcribe Figure E-2 in <a href={`${reference}newflexibledesignexample/`}>the flexible example</a>. A similarly named mix or a newer aircraft library may give different results. The numbers below are published manual results, not runs performed for this website.</p></DocCallout>
    <details open><summary>1. Design a flexible section, then verify its life</summary>
      <p>Follow <a href={`${reference}newflexibledesignexample/`}>Appendix E: New Flexible</a>, Figures E-1–E-16.</p>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li>Create a job named “Flexible practice” and a baseline section. Select US Customary units, New Flexible, Thickness Design, and a 20-year design life.</li>
        <li>Load the manual’s Design Examples traffic mix. Enter a 5-in. HMA surface, a starting 5-in. P-401/P-403 stabilized base, a P-209 aggregate layer, and subgrade CBR = 8. Check the full section against Figure E-3.</li>
        <li>Retain the example’s default options for the first run. Save and run. Record the final base and aggregate thicknesses and subgrade CDF.</li>
        <li>Copy the section. Enable Calculate HMA CDF and run again. Record subgrade CDF and fatigue CDF for both asphalt layers; identify which asphalt layer has the higher fatigue damage.</li>
        <li>Switch the completed section to Life without changing thicknesses or traffic. Run and compare the calculated life with the 20-year design target.</li>
        <li>Open the CDF Graph. Record the location of maximum total CDF and each aircraft’s contribution at that location. Explain why the heaviest aircraft need not govern.</li>
        <li>Run Life and Compaction. Read the separate cohesive and non-cohesive requirements and their depth references. Export the Section Report and CDF Graph; save the job.</li>
      </ol>
      <DocCallout label="Manual inconsistency" kind="note"><p>The text reports 18.8 in. of aggregate on the initial run and 18.5 in. after enabling HMA CDF, then returns to 18.8 in. in its final-design paragraph. Keep the report for your actual run and compare the figures; do not silently combine results from different runs. This is a documentation inconsistency, not a confirmed software bug.</p></DocCallout>
      <p><strong>Explain:</strong> Which criterion controls thickness? Which aircraft controls compaction? Why can these answers differ? Repeat with only subgrade CBR changed and describe what moved in the report.</p>
    </details>
    <details><summary>2. Design a rigid section</summary>
      <p>Follow <a href={`${reference}newrigiddesignexample/`}>Appendix E: New Rigid</a>, Figures E-23–E-30.</p>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li>Create a separate New Rigid section with the same complete traffic mix and 20-year design life.</li>
        <li>Use P-501 PCC with flexural strength 675 psi, an 8-in. P-304 cement-treated base, P-209 below it, and subgrade k = 120 pci. Copy all remaining properties from the example’s section figure.</li>
        <li>Confirm the PCC surface is the design layer. Save, run Thickness Design, and read concrete thickness and PCC CDF.</li>
        <li>Run Life and Compaction with the final thicknesses fixed. Compare life with the design target and inspect the report’s aircraft damage contributions.</li>
        <li>Copy the section, change only flexural strength, and rerun. Compare thickness, CDF, and governing aircraft. Restore the baseline before making another change.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The supplied manual reports a 17.0-in. PCC surface and PCC CDF = 1.0 for its original setup. Treat this as a versioned example, not a tolerance for a different aircraft library. Its rigid-example paragraph calls the graph a subgrade CDF; use the actual report’s PCC criterion and figure labels when interpreting the rigid design.</p>
    </details>
    <details><summary>3. Compare PCC and HMA overlays</summary>
      <p>Use the <a href={`${reference}pccoverlayonrigiddesignexample/`}>PCC overlay</a> and <a href={`${reference}hmaoverlayonrigiddesignexample/`}>HMA overlay</a> examples, Figures E-37–E-58.</p>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li>Rebuild the existing structure from Figure E-37; do not use the newly designed section from Exercise 2 as a substitute. Use the example’s traffic mix, SCI = 70, k = 120 pci, and 20-year overlay life.</li>
        <li>Make two copies. Add an unbonded P-501 PCC overlay to one and the specified HMA overlay to the other. For the PCC overlay, use flexural strength 650 psi.</li>
        <li>Check the pavement type, overlay design layer, and existing-layer condition. Note why percent CDF Used cannot be edited for this SCI in the example.</li>
        <li>Run each design and export its report. Compare overlay thickness, controlling criterion, and aircraft damage contributions. The manual reports 5.0-in. PCC and 7.6-in. HMA overlays for its respective cases.</li>
        <li>Explain why the reported existing-PCC modulus is not necessarily the effective modulus used throughout the deterioration calculation. If exploring a deteriorated base, use a separate copy and an explicitly stated condition assumption.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Limit:</strong> A thinner overlay is not automatically the better alternative. These calculations do not compare construction staging, drainage repairs, reflective cracking treatment, or project cost.</p>
    </details>
    <details><summary>4. Separate strength reporting from design</summary>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li>Use a saved completed example section. Record its thicknesses and traffic before switching to ACR/PCR.</li>
        <li>Follow the example’s PCR steps and enable Compute ACR for All Subgrade Categories when requested. Run and open the PCR Report, PCR Graph, and Form 5010.</li>
        <li>Identify the critical aircraft and decode the rating’s pavement type, subgrade category, tire-pressure category, and evaluation method.</li>
        <li>Compare the aircraft controlling PCR with those controlling design CDF and compaction. Explain why those are separate questions.</li>
        <li>Check the reporting procedure against current AC 150/5335-5D and its errata before using a rating beyond this exercise.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Read the full <a href={`${reference}pcrmode/`}>ACR/PCR mode instructions</a>. Do not interpret PCR as remaining life or use it to replace thickness design.</p>
    </details>
    <DocChecklist id="faarfield-exercises-v1" title="Keep with your exercise" items={['Saved baseline and clearly named alternatives.', 'Complete traffic mix, units, material inputs, and design options.', 'Software version and aircraft library date.', 'Section Report and CDF Graph for each comparison.', 'A short explanation of the governing criterion and the assumptions.']} />
  </section>
  <section id="assumptions" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Assumptions and limits</h2>
    <ul class="doc-list">
      <li><strong>Structural representation.</strong> Flexible analysis uses a layered elastic representation; rigid analysis uses a 3-D finite element model. A subgrade thickness of zero in the flexible report denotes an infinitely deep supporting layer, not missing soil.</li>
      <li><strong>Traffic.</strong> Forecast departures, growth, aircraft weights, gear, and lateral distribution are model inputs. A calculated life is conditional on those assumptions; it is not a guaranteed service date.</li>
      <li><strong>Material properties.</strong> Library properties and aggregate modulus assignment are part of the model. Use the applicable testing and FAA guidance for selecting design values; do not treat a default as a measured site property.</li>
      <li><strong>Existing pavement.</strong> Overlay results depend on condition and support estimates. A modeled SCI or modulus cannot replace a field survey.</li>
      <li><strong>Design completeness.</strong> Meeting a damage criterion does not by itself resolve minimum layer requirements, drainage, frost, constructability, joints, or rehabilitation details. Check the applicable FAA guidance separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Options and version.</strong> Advanced options change the calculation. Keep defaults for these exercises unless a step explicitly changes them, and record every change. See <a href={`${reference}advancedoptions/`}>advanced options</a> and <a href={`${reference}appendixddatafiles/`}>data files</a> for reproducible records.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="troubleshooting" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <DocCallout label="Bugs and version notes" kind="info"><p>No current FAARFIELD bug list was verified for this guide. The FAA download page links the release README; its download could not be retrieved during this source check. Check that file for your installed version before treating older release issues as current. The example-text discrepancies noted above are confirmed in the supplied manual, but are not evidence of a solver defect.</p></DocCallout>
    <p>If a run fails, preserve the job file, exact error message, calculation mode, options, software version, and aircraft library date. Try a copy of the smallest section that reproduces the issue. Use the support contact on the <a href={faarfieldDownload}>FAA page</a>; do not change engineering inputs just to suppress an error.</p>
    <details><summary>The wrong layer thickness changed</summary><p>Recheck the selected design layer and active section. Compare the input and final section in the report. See <a href={`${reference}designlayer/`}>design layer</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>The result changed after reopening the job</summary><p>Check the section, software version, aircraft library, and saved design options. Record the version and inputs with your report so a comparison can be repeated.</p></details>
    <details><summary>The job is taking longer than expected</summary><p>Read Calculation Status before restarting. Rigid designs use a 3-D finite element analysis and can take longer. See <a href={`${reference}solutiontimes/`}>solution times</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>I need to compare two designs</summary><p>Copy the baseline section and change one input. Keep traffic, design life, units, and other options fixed. Save the job and both Section Reports.</p></details>
  </section>
  <section id="manual" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>User’s manual</h2>
    <p>Browse the supplied FAARFIELD 2.1.1 help by chapter, or search its topic text. All 163 topics and their figures are included. Original technical text and version labels are retained.</p>
    <FaarfieldManual />
  </section>
  <section id="sources" data-section class="doc-section">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>For current software and guidance, use the <a href={faarfieldDownload}>FAA FAARFIELD page</a>.</p>
    <ul class="doc-list">
      <li><a href="https://www.faa.gov/airports/resources/advisory_circulars/index.cfm/go/document.current/documentNumber/150_5320-6">AC 150/5320-6G</a>: pavement design and evaluation.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.faa.gov/airports/resources/advisory_circulars/index.cfm/go/document.current/documentnumber/150_5335-5">AC 150/5335-5D and its errata</a>: ACR/PCR reporting.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="source-date">Sources checked October 1, 2026. The imported help is a versioned reference; current FAA guidance is linked above.</p>
  </section>
</SoftwareDocsLayout>
<style>
  details { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); padding: .75rem 0; }
  summary { cursor: pointer; font-size: .9375rem; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5; }
  summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent); outline-offset: 4px; }
  details p { margin: .75rem 0 0; }
  .source-date { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: .75rem; }
</style>
